{\"response\":30,\"explanation\":\"Using a binomial GLM of missing teeth out of scorable sockets with predictors for genus, age, sex, and tooth class (n=1450), Homo sapiens does not show higher AMTL frequencies than the non-human genera overall. The Homo vs Pan contrast is negative and non-significant (coef=-0.068, p=0.52), and Homo vs Papio is also negative and non-significant at 0.05 (coef=-0.161, p=0.084). Homo is significantly higher only relative to Pongo (coef=0.427, p=0.0057). Marginal predicted AMTL rates were ~0.065 for Homo, 0.070 for Pan, 0.076 for Papio, and 0.044 for Pongo, indicating Homo is similar to or lower than Pan/Papio and only higher than Pongo. Therefore, the evidence does not support a general claim that modern humans have higher AMTL than non-human primates after accounting for age, sex, and tooth class.\"}
